course: space
lesson: 19
title: How stars die
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: When a star like the Sun runs short of hydrogen in its core, it swells into a red giant hundreds of times wider; in about 5 billion years the Sun will do this, swallowing Mercury and Venus and possibly the Earth.
explain: Then it gently puffs off its outer layers as a glowing shell, a planetary nebula, and what is left is a white dwarf: about the size of the Earth but so packed that a teaspoon of it would weigh more than a car.
explain: Stars more than about 8 times as heavy as the Sun die violently in a supernova, an explosion that for weeks can outshine a whole galaxy, like a firework that briefly lights up a whole city.
explain: The collapsed core can become a neutron star, only about 20 km across, as wide as a city, yet heavier than the Sun; spinning ones sweep radio beams past the Earth like a lighthouse, and we call them pulsars.
explain: People have watched stars die: Chinese astronomers saw a "guest star" in 1054, a supernova blazed in 1987, and neutron stars crashing together, first seen in 2017, are thought to make much of the gold in the universe.
fact: When a star like the Sun runs short of hydrogen in its core, it swells into a red giant hundreds of times wider than before.
fact: In about 5 billion years the Sun will become a red giant, swallowing Mercury and Venus and possibly the Earth.
fact: At the end of its giant stage, a Sun-like star gently puffs off its outer layers as a glowing shell called a planetary nebula.
fact: What remains of a Sun-like star is a white dwarf, about the size of the Earth but with roughly half the Sun's mass, so a teaspoon of it would weigh several tonnes, more than a car.
fact: Stars more than about 8 times as heavy as the Sun end in a supernova, an explosion that for weeks can outshine a whole galaxy.
fact: In 1054 Chinese astronomers recorded a "guest star" bright enough to see by day for weeks, and its remains are the Crab Nebula in Taurus.
fact: In February 1987 a supernova in the Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy next to ours, became visible to the naked eye from the southern hemisphere, though not from northern lands like Britain; it was the closest seen since 1604.
fact: A collapsing core can become a neutron star, only about 20 km across but heavier than the Sun, so a teaspoon of it would weigh billions of tonnes.
fact: In 1967 Jocelyn Bell Burnell, a student from Northern Ireland, discovered pulsars, spinning neutron stars that sweep beams of radio waves past the Earth like a lighthouse.
fact: Much of the gold in the universe is thought to be made when two neutron stars crash together, an event first observed in 2017.
fact: Betelgeuse, Orion's red shoulder, is a dying red supergiant that will explode as a supernova sometime within about the next 100,000 years; nobody can say exactly when.
fact: A planetary nebula has nothing to do with planets; it got the name because it looked like a round planet in old telescopes.
exercise: What happens when a star like the Sun runs short of hydrogen in its core? | It swells into a red giant | It shrinks into a black hole at once | It explodes as a supernova | It turns into a planet
exercise: How much wider does a red giant get? | Hundreds of times wider than before | About twice as wide as it was before | No wider at all | Millions of times narrower
exercise: When will the Sun become a red giant? | In about 5 billion years | Next century | In about 5,000 years | Never, the Sun cannot change
exercise: Which planets will the red giant Sun swallow? | Mercury and Venus, and possibly the Earth | Jupiter and Saturn | All eight planets for certain, right out to Neptune | None, it will shrink instead
exercise: What is a planetary nebula? | A glowing shell puffed off by a dying Sun-like star | A cloud of gas and dust where new planets are being born | A cold planet covered all over in thick grey clouds of mist | The wide flat ring that goes round a giant planet like Saturn
exercise: What is left of a Sun-like star in the end? | A white dwarf about the size of the Earth | A neutron star about 20 km across, spinning fast | A black hole | Nothing at all
exercise: How heavy would a teaspoon of white dwarf be? | Several tonnes, more than a car | About as heavy as a teaspoon of sugar | Billions of tonnes | Lighter than air
exercise: Which stars end in a supernova? | Stars more than about 8 times as heavy as the Sun | Every star, including the Sun, ends with a huge explosion | Only the smallest red dwarfs, once they finally run out of fuel | Only stars that have planets going round them at the end
exercise: How bright can a supernova get? | For weeks it can outshine a whole galaxy | About as bright as a street lamp seen across the road | Never brighter than the full Moon on a clear night | Too faint to see with any telescope, even a big one
exercise: What did Chinese astronomers record in 1054? | A guest star bright enough to see by day | The first comet ever seen with two long bright tails | The discovery of Uranus | The first pulsar
exercise: What are the remains of the 1054 guest star? | The Crab Nebula in Taurus | The Pillars of Creation | The Pleiades | The Orion Nebula
exercise: What could people in the southern hemisphere see in February 1987? | A supernova in the Large Magellanic Cloud | Halley's Comet crashing into the Moon and lighting it up | A new planet near Neptune | A white dwarf swallowing Venus
exercise: How big is a neutron star? | About 20 km across, yet heavier than the Sun | About the size of the Sun | About the size of the Earth | About 20 metres across, and as light as a cloud
exercise: How heavy would a teaspoon of neutron star be? | Billions of tonnes | A few kilograms | Several tonnes | About as heavy as a car
exercise: Who discovered pulsars, and when? | Jocelyn Bell Burnell, in 1967 | Clyde Tombaugh, in 1930 | William Herschel, in 1781 | Galileo, in 1610
exercise: What is a pulsar? | A spinning neutron star sweeping radio beams past us | A planet that flashes like a torch | A comet with a pulsing tail | A white dwarf that beats like a heart, growing and shrinking
exercise: Where is much of the gold in the universe thought to be made? | When two neutron stars crash together | Deep inside the Earth's volcanoes | In the Sun's core today | In comets' tails
exercise: What is Betelgeuse? | A dying red supergiant in Orion | A white dwarf next to Sirius | A planet in another solar system | The Pole Star
test: Jack says Betelgeuse is gonna blow up tonight, he saw it on TikTok. what's true? | It will explode sometime within about 100,000 years; nobody knows when | It is sure to explode tonight | It exploded last year | It will never explode
test: Will the Sun end in a supernova? | No, it is not heavy enough; it will become a white dwarf | Yes, all stars explode at the end, whatever size they are | Yes, in about 1,000 years | No, it will turn into a planet
test: A pulsar's beam sweeps past the Earth 30 times a second. What does that tell you? | The neutron star spins 30 times a second | The star is 30 km wide and lights up once for each kilometre | Aliens are sending 30 messages a second to the Earth | The star is 30 years old and flashes once for every year
test: Two dead stars are compared: one is the size of the Earth, the other only 20 km across but heavier. Which is which? | The first is a white dwarf, the second a neutron star | The first is a neutron star, the second a white dwarf | Both are white dwarfs | Both are red giants
test: In 1054 people saw a star by day where there had been none. Why did they call it a guest star? | It appeared, stayed a while and faded, like a visitor | It came from another galaxy to visit our sky for a while | A guest at a feast saw it first | It was the Sun's partner star
test: bloody hell, my gold ring was made by crashing stars?? | Much gold is thought to come from neutron stars crashing together | No, all gold is made slowly in the Earth's rocks by heat and pressure | Yes, but only the gold that is found on the Moon, not on the Earth | No, gold is made by the Sun every day and falls to the Earth as dust
test: If the Sun became a red giant tomorrow (it won't), what would happen to Mercury? | It would be swallowed up | It would become a moon of the Earth | It would turn into a comet | It would be pushed out past Pluto
test: A cartoon shows a planetary nebula with planets inside it. what's wrong | It is a glowing shell of gas from a dying star, with no planets | Nothing, it is a nest where baby planets grow inside the gas cloud | It should be a single giant planet | It should show the Earth's clouds
test: Why could people in Britain not see the 1987 supernova with their own eyes? | It was in the Large Magellanic Cloud, seen from the southern hemisphere | It was too faint for anyone's eyes | It happened only in the daytime | It was hidden behind Jupiter
test: Which star would end as a neutron star or worse rather than a white dwarf? | One 15 times heavier than the Sun | The Sun | A small red dwarf | One half as heavy as the Sun
test: who found pulsars, some old professor? | No, a student, Jocelyn Bell Burnell, in 1967 | Yes, Isaac Newton found them in 1687 with his telescope | Yes, Galileo in 1610 | No, a space probe in 2017
test: A white dwarf weighs about half as much as the Sun but is only as big as the Earth. What follows? | Its matter is packed incredibly tightly | It must be hollow inside | It is made of light gas like a balloon | It must be cold and made of ice
exam: Identify the final remnant of a star of a mass comparable to that of the Sun. | A white dwarf | A neutron star | A supernova | A protostar
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning stars exceeding approximately eight solar masses. | They terminate their existence in a supernova | They become planetary nebulae and nothing further | They endure for trillions of years | They are transformed into planets
exam: State the name of the person credited with the discovery of pulsars in the year nineteen hundred and sixty-seven. | Jocelyn Bell Burnell | Clyde Tombaugh | William Herschel | Galileo Galilei
exam: Ascertain the remnant of the stellar explosion recorded by Chinese astronomers in the year one thousand and fifty-four. | The Crab Nebula | The Eagle Nebula | The Pleiades | The Large Magellanic Cloud
